  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 142 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nTuesday night through Monday:\n\nThe ridging aloft will remain in place across the central and \neastern CONUS through the rest of the work week keeping conditions \ndry. An overall benign and quiet forecast period. The next \nchances, while uncertain and weak, for rain is Saturday \nevening/Saturday night. The ridging aloft will start to tilt and\nbreak down a bit over the weekend as a low pressure system over\nthe Pacific Northwest starts to move east. The split among \nguidance is still fairly significant which you can see in the \ndeterministic runs of the global models well. This does continue\nthrough the ensembles and the cluster analysis which continues \nto show uncertainty in timing, location and strength of the \nlow. The GFS has the stronger and more progressive low, which \nthe EURO and CAN keep the low a bit weaker and have it as a \nridge rider. The track of this low will determine what comes \nnext for southern Wisconsin. Does the flow become a bit more \nmeridional or does it stay zonal, either way there does seem to\nbe a slight shift int he pattern after this Saturday. This \ncould bring some more chances for rain back into the forecast as\nwe look into next week.\n\nTLDR for Saturday at the sfc is the location of the low and how\nstrong the associated cold front is uncertain. Available \nmoisture looks to be fairly low at this point which means this\ncould be a dry frontal passage if there isn't any good WAA\nout ahead of the front. There is still too many uncertainties \nto draw any concrete conclusions so kept to the average among \nguidance (5-15% POPs with better chances as you go north into \ncentral and northern Wisconsin). The slightly warmer than normal\ntemperatures are expected to continue throughout the extended \nwith the prolonged southerly winds. \n\nPatterson",
